      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "A rail line once ran between Orange City and New Smyrna, stopping at what would become Samsula and nearby Indian Springs.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        21
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://samsula-womans-club.weebly.com/history-of-samsula.html",
      "category": "transport",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "Turpentine camps dotted the Samsula region, supplying Florida’s early naval‑store industry with essential resin.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        22,
        23
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://samsula-womans-club.weebly.com/history-of-samsula.html",
      "category": "economy",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "In 1912, Howe and Currier advertised in national papers, inviting settlers to own land and farm independently in Samsula.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        25,
        27
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://samsula-womans-club.weebly.com/history-of-samsula.html",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "The Sopotnick and Luznar families joined the community in 1914, helping shape Samsula’s early agricultural identity.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        28
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://samsula-womans-club.weebly.com/history-of-samsula.html",
